    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d60">

                <pb facs="0291" n="251" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v05/pg251"/>

                <head>60. Memorandum From the Secretary of State’s Special Assistant (Boster) to the Executive
                        Secretariat<anchor n="1" xml:id="d60-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-05-22"><hi rendition="#italic">May 22,
                        1958</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>There follows, for appropriate distribution, an excerpt from a conversation
                    between the President and the Secretary on May 22: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I discussed the prospective trip of Dr. Milton Eisenhower to Central America. I expressed the
                            view that it would be desirable to carry through on this visit, although
                            perhaps some slight alteration in the timing might be useful to avoid
                            labor and university gatherings. <pb facs="0292" n="252" xml:id="pg252"
                            /> The President said he would get in touch with Dr. Eisenhower along these lines.”<anchor
                                n="2" xml:id="d60-f2"/></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                    >DEB</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d60-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d60-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 120.1520/5–2258. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d60-f2" n="2" xml:id="d60-f2-content">
                <p>After this conversation with Secretary Dulles, President Eisenhower telephoned his brother and suggested that he inform
                    reporters, if asked, that he intended to make his trip to Central America but to
                    be vague about its timing. (Eisenhower
                    Library, Whitman File, Eisenhower
                    Diaries) During another conversation with the President on May 26, the Secretary
                    informed the President that the Department of State was trying to get an
                    accurate assessment of the current situation in each of the Central American
                    nations that Dr. Eisenhower planned to
                    visit. Dulles believed that Dr. Eisenhower should carry out the trip. He noted,
                    however, that the situation in Panama was sufficiently serious to warrant
                    cancelling that portion of the visit. (<hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>.,
                    Meetings with the President)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
